.he "ed (1)                       8/16/26                       ed (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
ed
- text editor
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
ed [ - ] [ name ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Ed is the line editor.  It reads the named file into a buffer, lets
you change it with commands, and writes it back only when you say so;
editing a file and quitting without writing leaves the file as it was.
With no name, ed starts on an empty buffer and the file must be named
with the e command.
.sp
A command is zero or more addresses, then one command letter, then
whatever the command takes.  Addresses name lines in the buffer; a
missing address is supplied by default, usually the current line.
Text is added to the buffer with a, c and i, and ended by a line
containing only a period.
.sp
Most commands take a range of two addresses separated by a comma.
The address before a command names the first line, the one after the
second; a single address is both.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
ADDRESSES
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
n	Line n, counted from the start; $ is the last line.

.ti -10
...	The current line.

.ti -10
/re/ and ?re?	The next or previous line containing the regular expression re.

.ti -10
'c	The line marked with k c.

.ti -10
+ and -	The current line moved that many lines; +3 or -2.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
COMMANDS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
a	Append text after the addressed line.

.ti -10
c	Delete the addressed lines, then accept text in their place.

.ti -10
d	Delete the addressed lines.

.ti -10
e name	Edit name, throwing away any changes not written.

.ti -10
f name	Remember name as the file name, or print it with no argument.

.ti -10
g/re/cmd	Run cmd on every line containing re; v does it on every line that does not.

.ti -10
i	Insert text before the addressed line.

.ti -10
j	Join the addressed lines into one.

.ti -10
kc	Mark the addressed line as c.

.ti -10
l and p	Print the addressed lines; l shows non-printing characters.

.ti -10
m addr	Move the addressed lines to after addr.

.ti -10
q	Quit; q refuses if changes would be lost, Q always quits.

.ti -10
r name	Read name into the buffer after the addressed line.

.ti -10
s/re/new/g	Substitute new for re; the trailing g changes every occurrence on the line.

.ti -10
t addr	Copy the addressed lines to after addr.

.ti -10
u	Undo the last substitution.

.ti -10
w	Write the buffer to the file; W appends.

.ti -10
=	Print the line number of the addressed line.

.ti -10
!	Run a subshell; leaving it returns to ed.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPTIONS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-	Suppress the explanatory output, the byte counts and the ? on an error, for use when the standard input is a script.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The regular expressions are the compact ones ed has always used, not
the extended set: ( ) for grouping and \\1 for a back-reference, with
no | or +.
.sp
The ! command runs a plain subshell.  v7 passed the shell a -t to ask
for one line; micronix sh has no such flag, so the shell stays up
until you end it, which is the one behaviour that differs from the
original.
.sp
A file being edited is kept in a temporary file under /tmp, so /tmp
must exist and be writable.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
grep (1), vi (1)
